# lemma-gate.expect — what `make leanlemma` must observe, declared.
#
# Read tools/lean-lemma.py's header for what this gate asserts and what it does not. The
# short version: `leanproof` grades KEYSTONE's proofs, this grades OURS, and the two are
# separate so a typo of ours cannot turn red the gate whose job is watching their tree.
#
# Row kinds:
#   gate|<full lean name>|<comma-separated axioms, or `-`>   the declaration's axiom set,
#        exactly, in both directions. Only names under `EntityCoreFormalization.` are
#        accepted -- a keystone declaration here is a parse error, not a silent pass.
#   eval|<exact string>                                      the #eval output, one to one:
#        an undeclared eval line fails exactly as a missing one does.
#   warn|<substring>|<why tolerated>                         a Lean warning is otherwise a
#        build failure (D13 -- a `sorry` is reported in the warning channel).
#   site|<repo-relative path>|<claim text, verbatim>         a document that publishes a
#        figure this sweep produces.

# ── declared warnings ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This tier builds OUR lemmas inside a COPY of keystone's tree, so keystone's own warnings
# reach it too — and the same one must therefore be declared in both expect files. It is not
# a hole: `lean/proof-gate.expect` grades that declaration's axiom set and it is standard.
# Routed to keystone 2026-09-15; DELETE THIS ROW WHEN THEY LAND IT, in both files.
warn|CapabilityProofs.lean:144:14: This simp argument is unused|keystone's, new at `fee2e422` in `matchesSegNM_trans`. Cosmetic; graded standard-axiom by the proof tier. Delete on their fix.

# ── the theorems ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# All four on Lean's standard axiom set; none carries `sorryAx`. The two that pull in
# `Classical.choice` do so through `simp`'s decidability instances, not through a classical
# step in the mathematics -- noted because the difference between these two lines and the
# other two is the kind of thing that reads as significant and is not.
gate|EntityCoreFormalization.A31.matchesSeg_starFree|propext, Quot.sound
gate|EntityCoreFormalization.A31.starFree_matches_only_itself|propext, Quot.sound
gate|EntityCoreFormalization.A31.matchesSeg_starFree_iff|propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound
gate|EntityCoreFormalization.A31.mp54_literal|propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound

# ── the differential ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every number the A-31 packet publishes, derived rather than recalled. `guardmisses` is the
# bridge check the header explains (star-free ⇒ §5.4's three guards all fail, checked over
# all 84 patterns because it is not proved); `controlmisses` is the seven-row control set,
# including §5.4's own two worked examples. Both must be 0 -- and note that a `0` here is
# the WEAKER kind of green: it says the sweep found nothing wrong, which is also what a
# sweep with no teeth says. That is what `neg-eval` is for.
eval|A31 pairs=3276 disagree=108 over=108 under=0 exhausted=0 starfree=1521/0 edgeonly=2652/0 interior=624/108 guardmisses=0 controlmisses=0
# The two K2 witnesses, so the finding itself reproduces and not only its summary.
eval|[/peerL/*/b]lean=true,spec=false [/peerL/x/*/z]lean=true,spec=false

# ── the published sites ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# D15: a derived number is a claim, and it goes stale in prose nothing re-reads. These are
# the LIVE documents that state a figure this sweep produces. The dated handoff is
# deliberately NOT here, for the reason `runcount` gives: a number this repo publishes is
# checked, a number it publishes about its own past is not, and making accurate history
# fail the build is how a gate teaches you to delete it.
#
# NOT COMPLETE, and nothing can make it so -- no tool derives the set of documents that
# quote a number. Adding a site is a hand edit, exactly as in runcount/enginecount.
site|docs/outbox/ROUTING-2026-09-09-KEYSTONE-SCOPE-SUBSET-TYPING.md|| pairs compared | **3276** |
site|docs/outbox/ROUTING-2026-09-09-KEYSTONE-SCOPE-SUBSET-TYPING.md|| **star-free** patterns (no `*` anywhere) | 1521 | **0** |
site|docs/outbox/ROUTING-2026-09-09-KEYSTONE-SCOPE-SUBSET-TYPING.md|| `*` only in the **first** segment or only as the whole **last** segment | 2652 | **0** |
site|docs/status/TRACKER-entity-system-architecture.md|**3276 pairs, 108 disagreements, all one-directional (Lean over-grants, zero under-grants)**
site|docs/status/TRACKER-entity-system-architecture.md|on **star-free patterns, 1521 of 1521 agree**

# ── the chain measurement (K2, lean/lemmas/Chain.lean) ──────────────────────────────────
# The three soundness questions and the chain question on top, over TWO domains — `general`
# and the peer-faithful `peerlike` — because the first draft's answer changed when the target
# alphabet did. Read them together:
#   ksSoundKS=0        keystone's check implies containment under keystone's own matcher.
#                      That is `matchesSeg_trans` lifted; a non-zero would contradict a
#                      theorem `make leanproof` grades.
#   specSoundSpec=0    §5.6's cover check implies containment under §5.4 — over `admit54`
#                      admitted pairs, which is why that count is on the same line.
#   ksSoundSpec>0      THE NON-VACUITY WITNESS. If this ever reads 0, every other zero in
#                      these rows is worthless and the file must be re-read, not re-declared.
#   escReal            of those, the ones whose PARENT authorizes real targets under §5.4.
#                      Was 0 in the first draft and that was the target alphabet, not the
#                      protocol (§1.4: a path segment may be literally `*`).
#   chainNew*=0        two admitted links reach nothing one link would not — and `reach2*`
#                      equals `admit*`, which is what stops that zero meaning "nothing
#                      composes".
eval|K2CHAIN[general] pats=84 tgts=84 ksSoundKS=0 specSoundSpec=0 ksSoundSpec=147 escReal=147 escParentInterior=147 escChildInterior=0 chainNewKS=0 chainNew54=0 chainEsc=0 admitKS=591/507 admit54=444/360 reach2KS=591 reach2_54=444
eval|K2CHAIN[peerlike] pats=26 tgts=13 ksSoundKS=0 specSoundSpec=0 ksSoundSpec=30 escReal=30 escParentInterior=30 escChildInterior=0 chainNewKS=0 chainNew54=0 chainEsc=0 admitKS=144/118 admit54=114/88 reach2KS=144 reach2_54=114
eval|[general] /a/a/a<=/a/*/a@/a/a/a(parent54=1) /a/a/a<=/a/*/*@/a/a/a(parent54=4) /a/a/a<=/*/*/a@/a/a/a(parent54=4)
eval|[peerlike] /p/a/a<=/p/*/a@/p/a/a(parent54=1) /p/a/a<=/p/*/*@/p/a/a(parent54=3) /p/a/a<=/*/*/a@/p/a/a(parent54=1)

# ── KS-9c: the frame-canonicalization equivalence (lean/lemmas/Frame.lean) ──────────────
# `entity-system-architecture` ROUTING-2026-09-12-e asked us to prove or refute that
# canonicalizing `resources` patterns at MINT is extensionally equivalent to canonicalizing
# at MATCH. The change is scheduled, so these numbers gate a decision rather than describing
# one. Read Q6 FIRST.
#
#   Q0   the kernel: a minted pattern re-canonicalizes to itself under ANY frame, including
#        an unrelated one. If this fails the two readings are different functions.
#   Q1/Q1x   L1/L2 `matches_scope`, include and exclude arms.
#   Q2/Q2x   L3/L4 `scope_subset` with per-link granter frames -- arch's item 2, the one they
#        said to take if we only took one, and item 4's exclude side.
#   Q3   the sentinel. `set-differs=0` is the real result: the patterns refused at mint are
#        EXACTLY the patterns unmatchable at match, so arch's item 3 ("same verdict by
#        different routes") holds at the matcher and the difference is only WHERE.
#   Q4   id-scope is frame-independent, measured rather than assumed (0.8.2.24 N2).
#   Q5   the alphabet-narrowing control. It reports 0 -- and so does every row above it, so
#        UNLIKE A-31's `neg-eval` it demonstrates nothing here. Kept, and labelled, because
#        a control that happens to be uninformative must not be read as one that passed.
#   Q6   THE POSITIVE CONTROL, and the row that makes every zero above mean something. Mint
#        against the WRONG frame -- §5.5a's own named `canon-against-wrong-frame` defective
#        architecture -- and the sweep MUST report non-zero. If Q6 ever reads 0/0 this file
#        is comparing nothing and Q0-Q5 are worthless.
#   Q7   THE QUESTION KEYSTONE'S TYPING CREATED (2026-09-15). `scope_subset` took a
#        `ScopeKind` at their `fee2e422`, so there is now an id branch at the L3/L4 layer
#        where before there was one untyped function. Mint-time rewrites an operand the id
#        matcher compares literally, so the two readings DIVERGE on `operations`/`peers`.
#   Q7b  Q7's ALPHABET CONTROL, and the row the finding actually rests on. `patterns` is a
#        path-scope operand list; narrowed to forms §3.6's id grammar admits, `over` falls
#        to 0 and only the fail-closed direction survives. ⛔ THE FIRST DRAFT OF Q7's NOTE
#        CLAIMED THE PERMISSIVE DIRECTION off the wide sweep, and this row withdrew it
#        within the hour -- the A-31 lesson (*the pair list of a differential is an input
#        set*) firing on the file that carries it. If Q7b ever reads 0 while Q7 does not,
#        the finding is about the alphabet and must be withdrawn, not re-declared.
eval|Q0 round-trip      pairs=90 fail=0
eval|Q1 L1/L2 include   pairs=240 disagree=0
eval|Q1x L1/L2 exclude  pairs=240 disagree=0
eval|Q2 L3/L4 include   pairs=900 disagree=0
eval|Q2x L3/L4 exclude  pairs=900 disagree=0
eval|Q3 sentinel        refused-at-mint=8 set-differs=0
eval|Q4 id-scope frame-independent  pairs=120 frame-sensitive=0
eval|Q5 control (plain alphabet)    pairs=64 disagree=0
eval|Q6 POSITIVE CONTROL wrong-frame mint  L3/L4=62 (MUST be > 0)  L1/L2=14 (MUST be > 0)
eval|Q7 id-scope L3/L4 mint≠match   pairs=900 disagree=166 over=114 under=52 (MUST be > 0 — see the note)
eval|Q7b id-plausible alphabet      pairs=144 disagree=26 over=0 under=26
eval|Q7b witnesses: pA/pA:*/apply<=*(match=true,mint=false) pA/pB:*<=*(match=true,mint=false) pA/pB:tree/get<=*(match=true,mint=false) pA/pB:tree/get<=tree/get(match=true,mint=false)
# The witness lines. Q0-Q2x are EMPTY because nothing disagrees; declaring them empty is the
# point -- if a disagreement ever appears the gate names it rather than moving a count by one.
eval|Q0 witnesses: none
eval|Q1 witnesses: none
eval|Q1x witnesses: none
eval|Q2 witnesses: none
eval|Q2x witnesses: none
eval|Q3 refused-at-mint set: pA|*/apply pA|../x pA|./x pA|*/secret pB|*/apply pB|../x pB|./x pB|*/secret

# ── A-4: what K-7 actually closed, and whether R11's witness still constructs ────────────
# `lean/lemmas/IdScope.lean`. `entity-core-keystone` ask A-4 says R11's withdrawn bound
# should be restored because its witness cannot be built. It COULD be built on the artifact
# our ledger pins when R11 was measured, and it cannot be built now -- and the two commits
# that closed it both landed AFTER the disagreement, neither of them being the argument A-4
# made. This gate holds that as four verdicts and three sweeps rather than as a paragraph.
#
#   r11Pre    THE POSITIVE CONTROL, and every other row on this line is a `false`. It is the
#             over-grant on `canonSegs`/`scopeSubset` @ keystone 97bf1a05, transcribed from
#             `git show` rather than reconstructed. If this ever reads `false` the file has
#             stopped measuring the thing it is named after and the three falses below mean
#             nothing -- exactly `neg-eval`'s demonstration, one file over.
#   r11Id     closed by the `ScopeKind` dispatch alone (their `fee2e422`).
#   r11Path   closed by `canonSegs`'s `*/` sentinel arm alone (their `0c4a537b`).
#             Two INDEPENDENT repairs: either one kills the witness, which is why the answer
#             to A-4 is neither a concession nor a defence.
#   r11Grant  the same pair through `grantSubset`, i.e. the path a real chain takes. A direct
#             `scopeSubset` call is a function chosen to make a point; this is the call site.
eval|A4 r11Pre=true r11Id=false r11Path=false r11Grant=false
#
#   incl      THE SWEEP. 3 of 144 pairs change verdict across K-7, partitioned exhaustively
#             by §3.6's own grammar: `starpos` (a `*` outside the two grammatical positions),
#             `slash` (a pattern carrying §5.4 path syntax -- §3.6:1035 names this class in
#             as many words), `other` (unexplained; MUST stay 0 or it is a result).
#   ⛔        `narrowed=2 widened=1`. The widening is real and is toward the grammar (`*`
#             matches any value, so the pre-fix refusal of `/abs/op <= *` was the defect) --
#             but "K-7 narrows the check" is FALSE of one of the three rows and the headline
#             is where that gets lost. Read both fields.
#   excl      THE EXACT TRANSPOSE of `incl`, by construction, not a second measurement. The
#             exclude arm evaluates the same matcher with operands swapped, so on a
#             single-pattern alphabet swept over all ordered pairs the tally CANNOT differ.
#             Declared and labelled because two identical lines read as corroboration.
#   plain     THE MUST-AGREE CONTROL -- 25 pairs of ordinary operation names, no star, no
#             slash, `disagree=0`. This is the alphabet every hand-tried example lived in,
#             and it is why F50 survived three spec revisions and a four-language cohort.
eval|A4 incl  pairs=144 disagree=3 narrowed=2 widened=1 starpos=2 slash=1 other=0
eval|A4 excl  pairs=144 disagree=3 narrowed=2 widened=1 starpos=2 slash=1 other=0
eval|A4 plain pairs=25 disagree=0 narrowed=0 widened=0 starpos=0 slash=0 other=0
# The three rows themselves, so the finding reproduces and not only its summary.
eval|A4 incl witnesses: tree/get<=*/get(pre=true,id=false,starpos) compute/apply<=*/apply(pre=true,id=false,starpos) /abs/op<=*(pre=false,id=true,slash)
